      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a19.30.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g19.27.14', 'g19.28.16', 'g19.30.4'],
          lemma: "τί ἄρα ἔσται ἡμῖν; … παλινγενεσίᾳ",
          title: "“What's in it for us?” — answered straight, then bent",
          body: "Peter's question is the one piety trains us never to ask aloud — *we left everything; what do we get?* — and the striking thing is that Jesus does not rebuke it. He answers it, lavishly and in order. First the Twelve specifically: **in the παλινγενεσία** — the ”regeneration,” the born-again-ness of the *world itself*, creation's own resurrection (this rare word's only gospel appearance: the faith's hope was never an escape from the world but the world made new) — they will sit on twelve thrones over the twelve tribes. A promise spoken, note the quiet horror, to a group that includes **Judas**: office guaranteed, occupancy not — the seat was real and he left it empty (Acts 1:20 says exactly this). Then everyone: whoever left houses, family, fields *for my name* receives **many times over** — and Mark's version dares to itemize the return as houses, brothers, sisters, mothers, children ”now in this age”: the hundredfold is not gold but the surrogate family of chapter 12, actual tables and actual people, plus eternal life on top. Renunciation, in this economy, is not loss nobly endured but the worst-kept secret of a staggering trade. And then, so the ledger cannot curdle into a new seniority system, the closing proverb bends the whole answer back: **many first will be last, and last first** — a warning aimed not at outsiders but at the very people just promised thrones. The next chapter opens with a parable about payday designed to make exactly that point sting.",
        },
      ],
